/** @page python_threads_and_stackguide Python Threading and Stack Management

    @tableofcontents

    This module implements support for multiple %Python interpreters along with multithreading by managing the %Python
    interpreter thread state manually.

    Additionally, the %Python recursion limit is also set automatically depending on the current thread stack size set
    in %Qore.

    This is done to avoid crashes due to stack exhaustion in combination %Qore and %Python code.

    To avoid \c RecursionError exceptions when mixing %Qore and %Python in multithreaded code, make sure the thread
    stack size is set to an appropriate size in %Qore (using \c set_default_thread_stack_size() before the %Python
    interpreter is created, for example); the %Python recursion limits are then calculated and managed dynamically
    according to the thread stack size and thread stack usage at the time of context switches to %Python.

    @section python_created_threads Threads Created by Python

    A thread created by %Python (ex: with \c threading.Thread, an executor pool such as
    \c concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor, or a native thread of an extension module) can call %Qore: functions,
    methods, constructors and callables imported from %Qore.  Such a thread becomes a %Qore thread the first time it
    calls %Qore, and stays one while its %Python thread state exists (normally until the thread ends):
    - the %Qore objects it creates are kept in its thread data, and %Qore resources it acquires (ex: a
      @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex") stay its own from one call to the next, as in a thread started by %Qore; it is
      counted by @ref Qore::num_threads() "num_threads()" while it is registered
    - the %Qore code it calls runs under the @ref sandboxing "sandbox" of the @ref Qore::Program "Program" whose
      %Python interpreter runs on the thread, as when that code is called on a %Qore thread
    - %Qore code called from %Python runs without the %Python GIL, so other %Python threads can run meanwhile

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%requires python

PythonProgram p("
import threading
from qore.__root__.Qore.Thread import Queue

def start(q):
    # the thread calls Qore: it is a Qore thread while it runs
    t = threading.Thread(target = lambda: q.push('from a Python thread'))
    t.start()
    return t

def join(t):
    t.join()
", "threads.py");
Queue q();
auto t = p.callFunction("start", q);
printf("%s\n", q.get());
p.callFunction("join", t);
    @endcode

    @subsection python_threads_daemon Daemon Threads

    With %Python 3.13 and earlier, a %Python interpreter cannot be finalized while a daemon thread, or a thread started
    with the \c _thread module, still runs in it: destroying the interpreter (ex: deleting a
    @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" object) then leaves it in memory, with the thread still running its
    code; a call from that thread to %Qore raises a %Python \c RuntimeError.  With %Python 3.14, the interpreter is
    finalized, and such threads exit when they next run.  The destruction of an interpreter waits for its threads that
    are not daemon threads, as %Python does.

    @section python_fork Processes Created with fork()

    The %Python interpreters of the module are %Python subinterpreters, in which %Python does not support \c fork():
    - \c os.fork() and \c os.forkpty() raise a %Python \c RuntimeError in them; processes can still be started, ex:
      with \c subprocess
    - a child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" can use %Python, unless another thread held the %Python
      GIL when the process was created: the GIL can never be released in the child, so a call to %Python there raises a
      \c PYTHON-FORK-ERROR exception, and if the thread that called @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" was called from %Python,
      the child cannot return to %Python code: it writes an error message to \c stderr and exits with status 1
    - in a %Python process that uses %Qore through the \c qoreloader module, @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" called from
      %Python code runs %Python's fork protocol, as \c os.fork() does: the functions registered with
      \c os.register_at_fork() run, and in the child, %Python knows that only the calling thread exists in it, so its
      exit does not wait for the threads of the parent
    - this is not done while %Python interpreters of %Qore exist in such a process
      (@ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" objects, or @ref Qore::Program "Program" objects using %Python): in
      the child of its fork protocol, %Python deletes all interpreters but its main one, which crashes the child
      (<a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/139759">CPython issue 139759</a>).  The child of
      @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" can then use the interpreters, but it keeps the %Python state of the threads of the
      parent: %Python's own exit in the child waits forever for the threads of the parent that are not daemon threads.
      For the same reason, \c os.fork() in such a process crashes the child while interpreters of %Qore exist.
    - in a %Qore process, the module initializes %Python while it holds the lock of the process environment (see
      @ref python_environment), so code that %Python runs while it initializes (\c sitecustomize, \c .pth files) must
      not create a process with \c fork()

    @section python_environment The Process Environment

    Changes that %Python makes to the environment (\c os.environ, \c os.putenv(), \c os.unsetenv()) are made with the
    environment functions of %Qore, under one process-wide lock with the changes and reads of %Qore; the copy of the
    environment that each %Python interpreter makes into \c os.environ when it is created is read under the same lock.
    As in %Python, \c os.environ is a copy: it does not see changes that %Qore makes with
    @ref Qore::setenv() "setenv()" after the interpreter was created; use @ref Qore::getenv() "getenv()" to read the
    current environment.
*/
